uto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8238b0bd-1624-4cbd-8a34-fc44254ec8eb_1080x1080.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GMsm!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8238b0bd-1624-4cbd-8a34-fc44254ec8eb_1080x1080.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" class=\"icon-noB79L\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Have a chat with Erin and Subba on the retreat program and details.</p><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://beingme.com.sg/power-and-pause&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Arrange a Personal Chat&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:&quot;button-wrapper&quot;}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary button-wrapper\" href=\"https://beingme.com.sg/power-and-pause\"><span>Arrange a Personal Chat</span></a></p><div><hr></div><p></p>",
  "body_text": "We often imagine balance as a life in which nothing difficult happens. \n\nBut a life with upekkha, translated as equanimity, is not absent of disruption. It is having the capacity to remain steady and grounded while everything continues to move. \n\nThe Pali word upekkha suggests “looking on steadily” — meeting each experience with clarity rather than indifference. \n\nPraise and blame, gain and loss, recognition and criticism, pleasure and pain: these winds are always blowing. \n\nEquanimity does not ask us to avoid them, suppress our feelings, or become emotionally distant. \n\nIt helps us notice when we are being pulled too strongly towards what we like, or pushed away by what we dislike.\n\nThis steadiness matters especially when we care deeply. Without it, generosity can become depletion, determination can become strain, and compassion can become burnout. \n\nWith equanimity, we can offer care without being overly fixated on outcomes we cannot control.\n\nThe practice begins in ordinary moments: noticing pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral experiences without immediately grasping, resisting, or drifting away. \n\nIn other words, we learn to stay present without being swept away.\n\nBalance is not standing still. \n\nIt is the quiet inner strength that allows us to respond wisely, offer care sustainably, and hold the results of our efforts a little more lightly each day.\n\n---\n\nAt Take A Pause Live on Insight Timer over 18-19 July 2026, we practiced cultivating equanimity towards pleasant and unpleasant experiences. We closed the session with a reading of the poem Me Imperturbe by Walt Whitman.\n\nMeditation Practice\n\nTap Play to Practice Together:\n\nThis post is free for some time, and then archived for paid subscribers to support a sustainable, long-term sharing of mindful content. Upgrade to receive lifetime access to the full archive and ongoing updates.\n\nShare\n\n---\n\nThe Wholehearted Woman Retreat\n\nA five-day mindfulness retreat into quiet wisdom and the light within\n\n1-5 October 2026\nSiem Reap, Cambodia\n*Early Bird Offer till 31 August 2026\n\nLed by Erin Lee\n\nJoin Erin at Angkor Wat\n\n---\n\nPower & Pause Retreat\n\nA high-touch program supported by structured processes, guided practices, and reflective assignments\n\n8 - 12 October 2026\nUbud, Bali\n\nLed by Subba Sattvic and Erin Lee\n\nHave a chat with Erin and Subba on the retreat program and details.\n\nArrange a Personal Chat\n\n---",
